; calldata_hole_is_in_range: a slot the host left unset still occupies its
; place in the calldata, so the slots after it keep their indices.
;
; spec/xqvm/ISA.md's INPUT row bounds the slot against the count the host
; fixed, and the entries of that sequence need not all be set. The Rust VM
; holds an unset entry as RegVal::Unset and counts it in calldata.len();
; xquad.vm._prepare_calldata_python dropped it, so the same [null, 5]
; arrived as two slots on one backend and one on the other. Reading slot 1
; returned 5 there and raised CallDataIndex here -- a fault-versus-success
; divergence on a calldata shape that is legal on both.
;
; The program completes, so xquad/tests/test_step_parity.py runs it through
; the xquad.vm wrapper on both backends as well as the two CLIs.

PUSH 1
INPUT r0         ; slot 1 is populated, and the hole at 0 does not shift it
PUSH 0
OUTPUT r0
HALT
